<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-net-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Net convergence
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Verify that the given net of points converges.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> F </m> be the collection of finite subsets of the natural numbers <m> \N </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          F = \setmid{ S \in \ps \of{ N } }{ S \textrm{ is finite} }
        </md>.
        
        <m> F </m> is partially ordered and directed both upwards and downwards by containment <m> \subseteq </m>. Consider the net <m> \of{ x_{ S } }_{ S \in F } </m> of real numbers <m> x_{ S } \in \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          x_{ S } = 2^{ -\max \of{ S } }
        </md>.
        
        Verify that <m> \of{ x_{ S } }_{ S \in F } </m> converges upwards to <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> N \in \Nbhd \of{ 0 } </m> be a neighborhood of <m> 0 </m> in <m> \R </m>. Then <m> \mB_{ \epsilon } \of{ 0 } \subseteq N </m> for some positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. The Archimedean principle implies that
        
        <md>
          n \gt \log_{ 2 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } }
        </md>
        
        for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. Let <m> S_{ N } = \set{ n_{ \epsilon } } \in F </m>. We observe that for all indices <m> S \supseteq S_{ N } </m>,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ x_{ S } , 0 } \amp = \abs{ 2^{ -\max \of{ S } } - 0 } = \abs{ 2^{ -\max \of{ S } } } = 2^{ -\max \of{ S } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq 2^{ -\max \of{ S_{ N } } } = 2^{ -n_{ \epsilon } } \lt \epsilon 
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> x_{ S } \in \mB_{ \epsilon } \of{ 0 } \subseteq N </m>. We conclude that the net <m> \of{ x_{ S } }_{ S \in F } </m> converges upwards to <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ N } }_{ N \in \Nbhd \of{ x } } </m> be a net of points in a topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> indexed by the neighborhood system <m> \Nbhd \of{ x } </m>. Verify that if <m> x_{ N } \in N </m> for all indices <m> N \in \Nbhd \of{ x } </m>, then <m> \of{ x_{ N } }_{ N \in \Nbhd \of{ x } } </m> converges downwards to <m> x </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> N \in \Nbhd \of{ x } </m> be a neighborhood of <m> x </m> in <m> X </m>. Since
        
        <md>
          x_{ M } \in M \subseteq N
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> M \subseteq N </m>, the net <m> \of{ x_{ N } }_{ N \in \Nbhd \of{ x } } </m> converges downwards to <m> x </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
